# Modeling trials within neurons and mice {#sec-multilevel-glmm}

























Two chapters ago I closed a result with a sentence I liked more than I should have: per second, hippocampal neurons fire at about the same rate whether a patient holds one, two or three pictures in mind (@sec-count-regression). The model behind it had a count with a denominator (an offset for the length of the window), a negative binomial for the noise, and a random intercept per neuron that I called a preview. Every lab has a colleague who hears a sentence like that and puts a hand up. *Flat on average, fine. But neurons are not interchangeable. How do you know there is no group that speeds up and a group that slows down, with the average being the two of them cancelling?* That is the first question of this chapter, and it is a fair one: an average is a statement about a population, and a population can hide anything.

The second question is a debt. In the chapter on behavior I fitted a Student-*t* to the log crossing times of the hanging wire and then admitted that I had counted 516 attempts as 516 independent observations when they came from 63 mice, and that the interval for the genotype effect was therefore too narrow (@sec-beta-robust-regression). I told you the repair would come later. Later is now.

The last two chapters taught you to add levels to a Gaussian model: a varying intercept for each mouse, a varying slope for each response, a correlation between them ([the introduction to this part](multilevel-intro.qmd#sec-multilevel-intro) has the vocabulary, and I will not repeat it). This chapter does the same for the families of the part on generalized linear models: counts, successes and robust times. The plan is to answer the two questions with data we already know, and on the way to pick up four ideas that matter more than any single model. The multilevel structure and the family are two independent choices (@sec-glmm-dials). In a model with a log or a logit link, the coefficient describes a typical neuron or mouse, which is not the average one, and mixing the two up is the easiest way to misreport a GLMM (@sec-glmm-marginal). A varying intercept in a logistic model and the beta-binomial of the last part are two descriptions of one overdispersion (@sec-glmm-wire-logit). And what you want to predict (a new trial, a new neuron, a new patient) decides which cross-validation is honest (@sec-glmm-cv).

## Flat on average, flat for everyone? {#sec-glmm-hook}

A word on the data first, because I changed my mind about something. In @sec-count-regression I used three of the 16 patients, one per version of the protocol, because the models were refitted every time the book was built and a fit on all 24,462 neuron-trials was too slow to ask of a laptop. Here the fits are cached, and I run them with threads (four chains, each spread over four processor cores), which makes each gradient evaluation about three times faster and changes nothing in the model. I use all 190 hippocampal neurons of the 16 patients of @kyzar2024, with 24,462 neuron-trials, which gives a true three-level structure (trials in neurons in patients) and lets me ask whether the conclusion about neurons survives the fact that neurons come from people. The protocol differences of @sec-count-regression (the pictures stayed on screen for about 1, 1.5 or 2.5 seconds depending on the patient) are still handled by the offset and, for everything else that differs between patients, by the patient level of the model. I will go back to the three patients near the end, because with few groups something instructive happens.

Before any model, here is every neuron (@fig-glmm_neuron_lines). For each neuron and each load I compute its firing rate as all its spikes over all its seconds, and I draw one line per neuron across the three loads, with the geometric mean of the neurons on top.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Firing rate of every neuron at each load: all its spikes over all its seconds
rate_tab <- aggregate(cbind(spikes, window_s) ~ neuron + load, data = spk, FUN = sum)
rate_tab$rate <- rate_tab$spikes / rate_tab$window_s

# A neuron with no spike at some load cannot be drawn on a log axis (count how many)
n_zero_rate <- length(unique(rate_tab$neuron[rate_tab$rate == 0]))
rate_pos <- rate_tab[rate_tab$rate > 0, ]

# Geometric mean over neurons at each load (the thick line)
gm <- aggregate(rate ~ load, data = rate_pos, FUN = function(x) exp(mean(log(x))))

# One thin line per neuron, the geometric mean on top
ggplot(rate_pos, aes(x = load, y = rate, group = neuron)) +
  geom_line(color = "grey55", alpha = 0.35, linewidth = 0.4) +
  geom_line(data = gm, aes(group = 1), color = "#0072B2", linewidth = 1.6) +
  geom_point(data = gm, aes(group = 1), color = "#0072B2", size = 3) +
  scale_y_log10(breaks = c(0.1, 1, 10)) +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = 1:3) +
  labs(x = "Pictures held in mind (load)", y = "Firing rate (spikes per second, log scale)") +
  Plot_theme +
  legend_none()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Firing rate of each of the 190 hippocampal neurons at one, two and three pictures (thin grey lines, log scale). The thick blue line is the geometric mean over neurons. Neurons differ about a hundredfold in baseline rate, and most lines run nearly flat.](multilevel-glmm_files/figure-html/fig-glmm_neuron_lines-1.png){#fig-glmm_neuron_lines fig-alt='Many thin grey lines spread vertically between about 0.1 and 25 spikes per second across three loads on a logarithmic axis. Most lines are almost horizontal; a few tilt up or down a little. A thick blue line near 1.5 spikes per second is almost horizontal.' width=624}
:::
:::








I drew @fig-glmm_neuron_lines as a line per neuron, not as a bar of the mean with asterisks, because the question is about *individual* neurons and a bar would show exactly one number and hide the other 190. This is the figure I wish I had shown in the last chapter. The design tip is general: when the claim is "on average", draw the units that the average is made of, and let the reader see how much room there is for disagreement.

::: {.callout-tip title="How to read this figure"}
Each thin line is one neuron followed across the three loads. Read the vertical position first: neurons sit anywhere from about one spike every ten seconds to more than twenty per second, so the lines are spread over two orders of magnitude, and that spread is the neurons' *baselines*. Then read the slope of each line: a line that is flat is a neuron whose rate does not depend on load, a line that rises is a neuron that speeds up, one that falls slows down. Finally compare the thick line with the thin ones. The question of the chapter lives in the difference between the two: if the thin lines fan out in both directions, the flat thick line is hiding something; if they stay close to it, the average is a fair summary. The mistake to avoid is to read a tilted line as a response: a neuron that fires once every ten seconds has a handful of spikes per load, and its line tilts by chance alone.
:::

Look at the lines before you read the next paragraph. They are far apart, they are mostly parallel, and a few tilt. The trouble is that I cannot tell, by eye, how many of those tilts are neurons responding and how many are the luck of counting. That is what a multilevel model is for: it separates the spread that is *real* from the spread that is sampling noise, and tells me the size of the real part. A neuron with few spikes gets a tilt that is mostly noise, and the model will treat it that way.

## Two dials, not one {#sec-glmm-dials}

Here is the picture I want you to keep for the whole chapter. Think of a mixing desk with two sliders. One chooses the *family*: how the outcome is distributed around its expected value (a count, a success, a time) and the link that connects that expected value to the linear predictor. The other chooses the *structure*: whether the observations are treated as strangers or as members of groups, and how many layers of groups there are. In the part on generalized linear models we moved the first slider and left the second alone. In the last two chapters we moved the second and left the first on Gaussian. Nothing prevents you from moving both, and nothing about one slider changes what the other one does. @tbl-glmm_map says it with the models of this book.








::: {#tbl-glmm_map .cell tbl-cap='Each model of the part on generalized linear models and the multilevel version that this chapter gives it. The family is the one that was already chosen; only the groups are new.'}

```{.r .cell-code}
# One row per model: the question, the earlier model and its multilevel version
map_tab <- data.frame(
  Question = c("Spikes per second under three loads", "Completed attempts on the wire",
               "How long a crossing takes", "NeuN-positive neurons in cortex"),
  `Single-level model` = c("Negative binomial with an offset", "Beta-binomial", "Student-t on the log time",
                           "Gaussian on the log density"),
  `Model with levels` = c("Negative binomial, offset, patients and neurons", "Logistic, one varying intercept per mouse",
                          "Student-t, one varying intercept per mouse", "Negative binomial, offset, mice and sections"),
  Formula = c("spikes ~ load + offset(log(window_s)) + (1 | patient) + (1 | neuron)",
              "success ~ genotype + (1 | mouse)",
              "log_wire ~ genotype + day + attempt + (1 | mouse)",
              "positive ~ dpi * hemi + offset(log(area_mm2)) + (1 | mouse) + (1 | section)"),
  check.names = FALSE
)
gt(map_tab) |>
  tab_style(style = cell_text(font = "monospace", size = "small"), locations = cells_body(columns = Formula))
```

::: {.cell-output-display}

