Read @tbl-glmm_map by columns. The first two say what we did before, the third says what we do now, and the fourth is the formula, in which everything left of the bars is what you already knew and the terms in parentheses are what the last two chapters taught. This is why I do not give a separate section to "the theory of generalized linear mixed models": the likelihood is the one you chose, the linear predictor has more terms, and the groups' deviations live on the *link scale*, which is the one place where something new happens. Keep an eye on that, because it is the source of the conditional and marginal business below.

![Neurons as musicians in an orchestra. Each plays at its own baseline volume (varying intercept) and answers the conductor in its own way (varying slope); the average volume of the orchestra can hide a louder and a quieter section.](images/multilevel-glmm_orchestra-baselines-and-responses.png){#fig-glmm-orchestra fig-align="center" width="90%" fig-alt="Watercolor sketch of an orchestra seen from above and behind the conductor, with faceless musicians in teal and ochre playing violins, a cello, a flute, a horn and timpani at music stands. A dashed teal outline groups the musicians on the left and a dashed ochre outline groups those on the right, with the conductor in the middle raising a baton."}

@fig-glmm-orchestra is the picture to keep for the whole chapter: every musician has a baseline volume of their own (a varying intercept) and answers the conductor in their own way (a varying slope), and the average volume of the orchestra can hide a louder and a quieter section.

## Trials in neurons in patients {#sec-glmm-spikes}

The model of @sec-count-regression, with its two new terms, reads as follows. The number of spikes of neuron $k$ of patient $j$ on trial $i$ is negative binomial, with a mean equal to the window length times a firing rate, and the log of the rate is a typical log rate, plus an effect of load, plus a deviation for the patient, plus a deviation for the neuron:

$$
\begin{aligned}
y_{ijk} &\sim \text{NegBinomial}(\mu_{ijk},\, \phi), \qquad \log \mu_{ijk} = \log(\text{window}_{ijk}) + \alpha + \beta_{\text{load}[i]} + p_j + u_{k}, \\
p_j &\sim \text{Normal}(0,\, \tau_{\text{patient}}), \qquad u_k \sim \text{Normal}(0,\, \tau_{\text{neuron}}).
\end{aligned}
$$ {#eq-glmm-spk}

The first line is @sec-count-regression. The second is @sec-multilevel-varying-intercepts, with neurons nested in patients in the way that sections were nested in mice (every neuron belongs to one patient, and I built an identifier that is unique across patients, so `(1 | patient) + (1 | neuron)` is the nested model, as before). The new thing to hold on to is where the deviations live: on the log scale, which is where the rate is multiplied, not added. A neuron with $u_k = 0.7$ fires twice as fast as the typical neuron, whatever the typical neuron's rate is.

### Priors on the firing-rate scale

I keep the priors of @sec-count-regression for the intercept, the load coefficients and the shape, and add one for each spread. The two standard deviations get an exponential distribution, as in @sec-multilevel-varying-intercepts, and the way to choose its rate is the book's way: draw from the priors and look at the outcomes, not at the parameters. @fig-glmm_prior_rates does that for the firing rate of a neuron at one picture. I draw a typical log rate from its prior, add a patient deviation and a neuron deviation drawn from their priors, exponentiate, and put the observed rates of the 190 neurons along the bottom as a rug.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# The priors alone: a typical log rate plus a patient deviation plus a neuron deviation
set.seed(1)
n_sim <- 20000
prior_lograte <- rnorm(n_sim, 0.5, 1.5) + rnorm(n_sim, 0, rexp(n_sim, 2)) + rnorm(n_sim, 0, rexp(n_sim, 2))
prior_df <- data.frame(log10_rate = prior_lograte / log(10))
share_ok <- mean(exp(prior_lograte) > 0.05 & exp(prior_lograte) < 50)

# The same simulation with an Exponential(1) for both spreads, to see why I did not keep it
prior_lograte1 <- rnorm(n_sim, 0.5, 1.5) + rnorm(n_sim, 0, rexp(n_sim, 1)) + rnorm(n_sim, 0, rexp(n_sim, 1))
share_ok1 <- mean(exp(prior_lograte1) > 0.05 & exp(prior_lograte1) < 50)
share_hi1 <- mean(exp(prior_lograte1) > 100)

# Observed rate of each neuron: all its spikes over all its seconds
obs_rate <- aggregate(cbind(spikes, window_s) ~ neuron, data = spk, FUN = sum)
obs_rate$log10_rate <- log10(obs_rate$spikes / obs_rate$window_s)

# Prior density with the observed neurons as a rug
ggplot(prior_df, aes(x = log10_rate)) +
  geom_density(fill = "grey85", color = "grey40") +
  geom_rug(data = obs_rate, color = "#0072B2", alpha = 0.6, length = grid::unit(0.06, "npc")) +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = -2:3, labels = c("0.01", "0.1", "1", "10", "100", "1000"), limits = c(-3, 3.5)) +
  labs(x = "Firing rate (spikes per second, log scale)", y = "Prior density") +
  Plot_theme
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Firing rate of a neuron at one picture implied by the priors alone (density, log scale), with the observed rate of each of the 190 neurons as ticks along the bottom. The priors allow rates from a few hundredths to a few tens of spikes per second and rule out the absurd.](multilevel-glmm_files/figure-html/fig-glmm_prior_rates-1.png){#fig-glmm_prior_rates fig-alt='A smooth bell-shaped density over a logarithmic axis from 0.01 to 1000 spikes per second, centered near 1.6, with a long tail on both sides. Short ticks along the bottom, one per neuron, lie between 0.08 and 27 spikes per second, inside the bulk of the density.' width=624}
:::
:::








@fig-glmm_prior_rates is a prior predictive check in the sense of @sec-priors-regularization, drawn with a density and a rug because what I need to see is whether the observed neurons fall where the prior puts its mass: they do, in the middle of it, and 94% of the simulated neurons fall between 0.05 and 50 spikes per second, the range the original paper reports. With an Exponential(1) for both spreads, the choice of @sec-count-regression for the neurons alone, that share drops to 86% and 4.4% of the simulated neurons fire above 100 spikes per second, which with three levels of uncertainty stacked on top of each other I judged too generous. An Exponential(2) has a mean of 0.5 on the log scale, which is a factor of about 1.6 per standard deviation, and the data are free to say much more than that (they will). I check this choice at the end of the chapter, with a narrow and a wide prior.

### The fit, and what it costs

I fit two models, the one that ignores the levels (the model of @sec-count-regression, now with all 16 patients) and the one with a varying intercept for each patient and for each neuron.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Negative binomial that ignores neurons and patients, all 16 patients (the model of the chapter on counts)
fit_spk_pooled <- brm(
  spikes ~ load_f + offset(log(window_s)),
  data = spk, family = negbinomial(), prior = prior_spk[prior_spk$class != "sd", ],
  chains = 4, cores = 4, seed = 123, init = 0,
  file = "models/multilevel-glmm_fit1.rds", file_refit = "never"
)
```
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Trials in neurons in patients: a varying intercept for each patient and for each neuron
fit_spk_ml <- brm(
  spikes ~ load_f + offset(log(window_s)) + (1 | patient) + (1 | neuron),
  data = spk, family = negbinomial(), prior = prior_spk,
  chains = 4, cores = 4, threads = threading(4), seed = 321, init = 0,
  iter = 6000, control = list(metric = "dense_e", adapt_delta = 0.99),
  file = "models/multilevel-glmm_fit2.rds", file_refit = "never"
)
```
:::








A plain `glm.nb()` is all the frequentist side needed for the pooled model, and for the model with levels it is `glmmTMB` [@brooks2017], which handles the offset, the negative binomial and the nesting at once (`lme4::glmer.nb()` [@bates2015] fits the same model but is slower and less stable on data like these). `brms` gives me the full posterior of every spread, which is what I need to say how many neurons *could* respond, and it carries the uncertainty of those spreads into every coefficient.

