### Where the variation lives {#sec-glmm-variance}

A multilevel model is also a way of asking where the variation is, and for a count with a log link the answer needs one new idea, because the model has two kinds of scale. The counts live on the *observed* scale: a number of spikes in a window. The linear predictor lives on the [latent scale]{style="color:green; font-weight:bold;"}: the log of the rate, before any counting happens. Think of a bakery. The number of loaves sold on a given morning depends on how busy the bakery is and on the luck of who happens to walk in. How busy it is, on a log scale, is the latent quantity, and the luck of the walk-ins is what the counting adds on top. When I ask how much of the variation is between patients, between neurons and between trials, I ask it about the *busyness*, and I leave the luck of the counting out. On that scale the variance has three parts: the variance of the patients' deviations, $\tau_{\text{patient}}^2$; the variance of the neurons' deviations within a patient, $\tau_{\text{neuron}}^2$; and the wobble of the rate from one trial of a neuron to the next, which the negative binomial writes with its shape. If the rate of a trial is the neuron's rate times a gamma-distributed multiplier with shape $\phi$, the log of that multiplier has variance $\psi_1(\phi)$, the trigamma function, which shrinks as $\phi$ grows [@nakagawa2017]. That is the exact variance for a gamma multiplier. A delta-method approximation, $\log(1 + 1/\mu + 1/\theta)$, also circulates and depends on the mean rate, which is why I prefer the trigamma here: the partition then does not change with the firing rate of the neuron you have in mind. For the logistic model, the standard value is $\pi^2/3 \approx 3.29$ [@inchausti2023; @hardy2004; @nakagawa2017].

![The busyness of a bakery is the latent quantity; the luck of who walks in is what the counting adds on top.](images/multilevel-glmm_bakery-busyness-and-walk-ins.png){#fig-glmm-bakery fig-align="center" width="80%" fig-alt="Watercolor sketch of a bakery shop front with a striped teal awning and loaves in the window. A round dial on the wall in teal and ochre points to the busy half, with a dashed arrow to the door, where a queue of faceless silhouettes waits and the first is stepping inside."}

In @fig-glmm-bakery the dial on the wall is the latent quantity and the customers at the door are the counting.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Variances on the log-rate scale, from every posterior draw, and their shares of the total
v_pat <- dr_ml$sd_patient__Intercept^2
v_neu <- dr_ml$sd_neuron__Intercept^2
v_trial <- trigamma(dr_ml$shape)
v_tot <- v_pat + v_neu + v_trial

share_spk <- data.frame(
  level = rep(c("Between patients", "Between neurons\n(same patient)", "Between trials\n(same neuron)"), each = length(v_tot)),
  share = c(v_pat, v_neu, v_trial) / rep(v_tot, 3)
)
share_spk$level <- factor(share_spk$level, levels = rev(unique(share_spk$level)))

# Two intraclass correlations on the latent scale
icc_patient <- v_pat / v_tot
icc_neuron <- (v_pat + v_neu) / v_tot
share_neuron <- v_neu / v_tot
share_trial <- v_trial / v_tot
```
