Of the variation in log firing rate, 59% lies between neurons of the same patient, 32% between patients and 9% between trials of one neuron. Two neurons of the same patient share 32% of their variance, and two trials of the same neuron share 91%: these are the two [intraclass correlations]{.ix ix="intraclass correlation"} of this model, and they are on the latent scale (the scale of the log rate), so they say how alike two *rates* are, not two counts. Both are high, which is why intervals that treat trials as independent cannot be trusted, and the first has a consequence for how to read the whole recording: a third of what makes a neuron fire fast or slow is *which person it lives in*, so a study that adds neurons from the same three people is adding rows, not evidence about people.

## Does every neuron respond to load in its own way? {#sec-glmm-slopes}


To answer my colleague I let each neuron have its own response to load, and I let the data say how different those responses are. Two decisions come first, and I owe you the reasons.

The first is how to code load. In @sec-count-regression load was a factor, so that two pictures and three pictures each had their own coefficient. A varying effect for a factor would give every neuron *two* responses and a $3 \times 3$ covariance matrix among its baseline and its two responses, with about 45 trials behind each cell of each neuron. That is a lot of parameters for the data to support, and it asks a more complicated question than the colleague asked. The question was "does this neuron go up or down as the load grows", and that is one number per neuron: a slope. So I code the load as the number of pictures *beyond the first* (0, 1, 2), which makes the intercept the log rate of a neuron with one picture on screen, exactly as before, and the slope the log rate ratio *per extra picture*. Is it fair to assume a straight line on the log scale? At the population level I can check, because the model above has a coefficient for each load: a straight line says that the coefficient for three pictures is twice the one for two, and the difference between the two, $\beta_3 - 2\beta_2$, has a posterior of 0.005 [-0.029, 0.040], which does not exclude zero. Whatever curvature there is, it is smaller than I can see.

The second is the prior. The standard deviation of the slopes is a number I expect to be much smaller than that of the baselines, since baselines differ a hundredfold and a response of that size would be extraordinary, so I give it an Exponential(4) (a mean of 0.25 on the log scale, a change of about 28% in the rate per picture) against the Exponential(2) of the baselines. The correlation gets the LKJ(2) prior of @sec-multilevel-varying-slopes. The model, written for one neuron $k$ of patient $j$, adds two things to @eq-glmm-spk, a slope $s_k$ times the number of extra pictures, correlated with the neuron's baseline:

$$
\log \mu_{ijk} = \log(\text{window}_{ijk}) + \alpha + \beta\, x_i + p_j + u_k + s_k\, x_i, \qquad
\begin{pmatrix} u_k \\ s_k \end{pmatrix} \sim \text{MVNormal}\!\left(\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix},\, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}\right),
$$ {#eq-glmm-slope}

with $x_i$ the number of pictures beyond the first and the patient deviation $p_j$ as before. In `brms` the only change is one term: `(1 + pics | neuron)`.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Add a varying slope of load (pictures beyond the first) for each neuron, correlated with its baseline
fit_spk_slope <- brm(
  spikes ~ pics + offset(log(window_s)) + (1 | patient) + (1 + pics | neuron),
  data = spk, family = negbinomial(), prior = prior_spk_slope,
  chains = 4, cores = 4, threads = threading(4), seed = 123, init = 0,
  iter = 4000, control = list(metric = "dense_e", adapt_delta = 0.99),
  file = "models/multilevel-glmm_fit3.rds", file_refit = "never"
)
```
