```{.r .cell-code}
# Posterior draws of the slope model
dr_sl <- as_draws_df(fit_spk_slope)
b_pics <- dr_sl$b_pics
tau_s <- dr_sl$sd_neuron__pics
```
:::








The answer to the colleague is in one number, the standard deviation of the neurons' slopes: 0.037 [0.006, 0.058] on the log scale, per extra picture. Translate it: a neuron one standard deviation above the average has a rate that is about 4% higher per extra picture than the average neuron, and 2% of neurons would change their rate by more than 10% per picture. The population slope is 0.988 [0.975, 1.001] as a rate ratio per picture. So neurons may differ in how they respond (the lower end of the interval is close to zero, so the data cannot exclude that they hardly differ at all), there is room for a group that speeds up and a group that slows down, and the room is small: a typical spread of a few percent per picture, against baselines that differ a hundredfold. The correlation between a neuron's baseline and its response is 0.01 [-0.37, 0.48], wide and centered near zero, which is what one would expect from a spread of slopes this small: there is little response to correlate with anything.

Here is the picture that makes the answer credible (@fig-glmm_slopes). On the left, for every neuron, its own slope estimated from its own trials alone (a quasi-Poisson fit to that neuron, no pooling) against the slope the multilevel model gives it. On the right, the posterior of the spread of slopes against its prior and against the spread that a spreadsheet would show.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Raw slope of every neuron: its own quasi-Poisson fit with the window as offset, no pooling
raw_sl <- do.call(rbind, lapply(split(spk, spk$neuron), function(d) {
  s <- summary(glm(spikes ~ pics + offset(log(window_s)), family = quasipoisson, data = d))$coefficients
  data.frame(neuron = as.character(d$neuron[1]), raw = s["pics", 1], se = s["pics", 2], n_spikes = sum(d$spikes))
}))

# Partially pooled slope of every neuron: population slope plus its own deviation, from every draw
pooled_draws <- sapply(raw_sl$neuron, function(id) b_pics + dr_sl[[paste0("r_neuron[", id, ",pics]")]])
raw_sl$pooled <- apply(pooled_draws, 2, median)
raw_sl$p_lo <- apply(pooled_draws, 2, quantile, probs = 0.025)
raw_sl$p_hi <- apply(pooled_draws, 2, quantile, probs = 0.975)

# Neurons whose partially pooled 95% interval excludes zero, and the spread of the raw slopes
n_excl <- sum(raw_sl$p_lo > 0 | raw_sl$p_hi < 0)
sd_raw <- sd(raw_sl$raw)
se_raw <- median(raw_sl$se)
```
:::

::: {.cell}

