:::
:::








I used a scatter of raw against pooled estimates, with the diagonal and the population slope drawn in, because it is the simplest way to *see* shrinkage on 190 units at once: a point on the diagonal was left as it was, a point on the horizontal line was replaced by the population value. It is the arrows of @fig-neun_shrinkage drawn for too many units to need arrows. A bar chart of the neurons whose slope is "significant" is what a spreadsheet would give you, and it would be mostly noise.

::: {.callout-tip title="How to read this figure"}
On the left, the horizontal axis is what each neuron says about itself and the vertical axis is what the model believes about it after hearing from all the others. If the points hugged the diagonal, the model would be taking each neuron at its word. They do not: they form a nearly flat band, which says that the spread of raw slopes is mostly sampling noise, and that the model lends each neuron the information of the rest. On the right, read the position and the width of the shaded density against the dotted line: the real spread is a fraction of the spread the neurons *appear* to have. The mistake to avoid is to read the extreme points of the left panel as responders: the most tilted raw slopes belong to neurons with few spikes.
:::

The raw slopes of the neurons have a standard deviation of 0.12, and the typical standard error of one neuron's slope is 0.08: a spreadsheet would conclude that neurons differ in their response by about 13% per picture, when nearly all of that is the counting noise of each neuron (an error of 0.08 per neuron accounts for most of a spread of 0.12). The model's estimate of the real spread is 0.04. After partial pooling, 2 of the 190 neurons have a 95% interval for their slope that excludes zero. I want to be careful about what I do and do not conclude from that. I do not conclude that no hippocampal neuron responds to load: the model says that, in these recordings, the *spread* of responses across neurons is small, which constrains how many neurons can be responders and how strong their responses can be, within a straight-line response over the encoding window. A neuron with a rate that rises with the second picture and falls with the third would have a flat slope, and a response confined to a few neurons would not move a standard deviation much. The same caution as in @sec-multilevel-varying-slopes applies to every shrunken estimate here: the partially pooled slope of one neuron is the model's best guess for that neuron, not a measurement of it.









::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Maximum-likelihood fit with a varying slope for each patient too
glmm_spk_slope_pat <- glmmTMB(spikes ~ pics + offset(log(window_s)) + (1 + pics | patient) + (1 + pics | neuron),
                              family = nbinom2, data = spk)
```
:::








Does the answer survive the patients? The slope model above lets the baseline vary between patients but forces the same response for every patient. The question of whether the *population* effect of load is the same for everyone is a varying slope at the patient level, and with 16 patients and a spread of slopes this small a Bayesian fit of it would take hours for an answer I can get in seconds from `glmmTMB`, so I use that for this check and say so (the fit of the neuron slopes above is the one that matters for the question of the chapter). Adding `(1 + pics | patient)` to the maximum-likelihood model gives a spread of the load slope between patients of 0.016, against 0.035 between neurons within a patient, and a population slope of -0.014 per picture on the log scale, against -0.012 without the patient slopes. Patients differ in their response about as little as neurons do, and the population conclusion does not move; the standard error of the population slope grows from 0.007 to 0.0083, which is what a population effect should do when it is allowed to differ between the people it is measured on.

::: {.callout-tip title="Frequentist equivalent"}
`glmmTMB` fits the slope model in seconds and agrees with `brms` on what matters: a population slope of -0.012 per picture on the log scale against -0.012, and a spread of slopes between neurons of 0.04 against 0.037. Both are doing partial pooling by the same mechanism, which the opener drew. The difference is in what each says about the quantities with little information. The correlation between baseline and slope is estimated by maximum likelihood as -0.01, a point with no interval attached, while the Bayesian fit gives a whole posterior that is, correctly, almost as wide as the prior; and with a spread of slopes this close to zero a maximum-likelihood fit can land on the boundary and warn of a singular fit (here it did not). The standard errors that `glmmTMB` reports for the population slope treat the spreads as known, and in the model with patient-level slopes, where the spread between patients is estimated from 16 patients, that is the optimistic part.
:::

## The coefficient is not the average {#sec-glmm-marginal}

There is one more thing to settle before trusting any number in this chapter, and it is the most common way a multilevel GLM gets misreported. I said above that the intercept is the log rate of "a typical neuron". Look at the word. A varying intercept is a deviation from the intercept, and the intercept is where the deviations are zero, which is the neuron that sits exactly at the middle of the log-rate scale. Think of a classroom where a teacher asks for "the typical height". The median child is a real child with a real height, but the *average* height of the class is not the height of that child, if the class has a few very tall students: averaging a quantity that is skewed gives something bigger than the middle one. Firing rates are exactly that: neurons differ by multiplication, so the rates are skewed, and the neuron at the middle of the log scale fires more slowly than the average neuron.

![The median child is a real child; the average height of the class sits above her because of a few tall students. A conditional effect speaks about the first, a marginal effect about the second.](images/multilevel-glmm_median-child-and-class-average.png){#fig-glmm-class fig-align="center" width="85%" fig-alt="Watercolor sketch of nine faceless schoolchildren in a row, from the smallest on the left to the tallest on the right, with the child in the middle outlined in teal and a dashed ochre line across the whole row at the class's average height, above the outlined child's head."}

@fig-glmm-class pictures the difference: the conditional effect speaks about the highlighted child, the marginal one about the dotted line.

The statistics has two names for the two readings. A [conditional effect]{style="color:green; font-weight:bold;"} (also called subject-specific) is the effect for a neuron or mouse at a given value of its deviation, usually zero: it is what the coefficient of a GLMM means. A [marginal effect]{style="color:green; font-weight:bold;"} (population-averaged) is the effect averaged over the whole population of neurons or mice: it is what a paper's reader usually has in mind. With an identity link and Gaussian noise they coincide, which is why you did not meet the distinction in the previous two chapters. With a log or a logit link they do not, because averaging a curved function over a spread of groups is not the same as evaluating it at the average group (the point of @sec-multilevel-varying-slopes about the average mouse and a new mouse). McElreath makes the same distinction when he predicts for an actor with an average intercept and, separately, for the marginal of actors [@mcelreath2020]. The books say the same in two sentences that are worth memorizing. In a logistic model with a varying intercept the conditional coefficient is *larger in magnitude* than the marginal one, because the S-shaped curve is flattened when it is averaged over a spread of groups (Jensen's inequality at work). In a model with a log link and a varying intercept the *rate ratio* is the same in both readings, because the average over groups multiplies the numerator and the denominator by the same constant, $\exp(\sigma^2/2)$, which cancels; levels and differences are not so lucky [@gelman2020]. And the advice for reporting is to give predictions on the natural scale (probabilities, counts) averaged over the uncertainty of the varying effects, and to say explicitly whether a contrast is conditional (a unit with zero deviation) or marginal [@gelman2020; @gelman2026].

For the spike model the algebra is friendly, because a Gaussian deviation on the log scale gives a lognormal rate, and the mean of a lognormal is its median times $\exp(\sigma^2/2)$. With the draws of the slope model I can write all the versions down. Let $x$ be the number of pictures beyond the first; the typical neuron has rate $\exp(\alpha + \beta x)$, and the average over neurons and patients has rate $\exp\!\big(\alpha + \beta x + \tfrac{1}{2}(\tau_p^2 + \tau_u^2 + 2\rho\tau_u\tau_s x + \tau_s^2 x^2)\big)$. I also compute the average over the neurons we studied, in the way of the average predictive comparisons of @sec-logistic-regression, by asking the fitted model for the expected rate of every trial with the number of pictures set to one and then to three, with each neuron's own estimated baseline, and averaging over trials.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Draws of the pieces of the slope model
a <- dr_sl$b_Intercept
tau_p <- dr_sl$sd_patient__Intercept
tau_u <- dr_sl$sd_neuron__Intercept
rho <- dr_sl$cor_neuron__Intercept__pics

# Rate (spikes per second) with x extra pictures: for the typical neuron and averaged over neurons and patients
rate_typical <- function(x) exp(a + b_pics * x)
rate_average <- function(x) exp(a + b_pics * x + (tau_p^2 + tau_u^2 + 2 * rho * tau_u * tau_s * x + tau_s^2 * x^2) / 2)

# Average over the neurons we studied: expected rate of every trial under one and three pictures
draw_ids <- round(seq(1, ndraws(fit_spk_slope), length.out = 500))
rate_studied <- function(x) {
  e <- posterior_epred(fit_spk_slope, newdata = transform(spk, pics = x), draw_ids = draw_ids)
  rowMeans(sweep(e, 2, spk$window_s, "/"))
}
r1_studied <- rate_studied(0)
r3_studied <- rate_studied(2)

# One table of draws for the figure: rate with one picture, and the change from one to three pictures
marg_df <- rbind(
  data.frame(panel = "Rate, one picture (spikes/s)", who = "Typical neuron", value = rate_typical(0)),
  data.frame(panel = "Rate, one picture (spikes/s)", who = "Neurons studied", value = r1_studied),
  data.frame(panel = "Rate, one picture (spikes/s)", who = "Average over all neurons", value = rate_average(0)),
  data.frame(panel = "Change, one to three pictures (spikes/s)", who = "Typical neuron",
             value = rate_typical(2) - rate_typical(0)),
  data.frame(panel = "Change, one to three pictures (spikes/s)", who = "Neurons studied",
             value = r3_studied - r1_studied),
  data.frame(panel = "Change, one to three pictures (spikes/s)", who = "Average over all neurons",
             value = rate_average(2) - rate_average(0))
)
marg_df$who <- factor(marg_df$who, levels = rev(c("Typical neuron", "Neurons studied", "Average over all neurons")))
marg_df$panel <- factor(marg_df$panel, levels = c("Rate, one picture (spikes/s)", "Change, one to three pictures (spikes/s)"))
```
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Point and interval for each reading, one panel per quantity
ggplot(marg_df, aes(x = value, y = who)) +
  stat_pointinterval(.width = c(0.5, 0.95), point_size = 2.5) +
  facet_wrap(~ panel, scales = "free_x") +
  labs(x = NULL, y = NULL) +
  Plot_theme +
  theme(strip.text = element_text(size = 11, face = "bold"))
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
