:::
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Numbers quoted in the text: ratios per picture, three ways
ratio_typical <- exp(b_pics)
ratio_means <- rate_average(1) / rate_average(0)
ratio_mean_of_ratios <- exp(b_pics + tau_s^2 / 2)
```
:::








I drew @fig-glmm_spk_marginal as intervals on the *natural* scale (spikes per second), because the whole point is to compare numbers a reader can picture, and the log scale would hide a factor of two. Read each panel as three answers to three questions. On the left, the typical neuron fires 1.5 [1.0, 2.3] spikes per second with one picture on screen, and the average neuron of the population 3.4 [2.2, 6.5]: about twice as fast, because a few fast neurons pull the average up (the neurons we studied sit near the second, 2.9 [2.9, 3.0]). On the right, the change in the rate from one picture to three is -0.04 [-0.09, 0.00] spikes per second for the typical neuron and -0.07 [-0.21, 0.02] for the average one: the same effect, in absolute terms, differs by a factor of about 2 depending on whom you ask. A ratio is much steadier: the ratio of rates per picture is 0.988 [0.975, 1.001] for the typical neuron and 0.989 [0.974, 1.002] for the ratio of the average rates, because with a log link and a small spread of slopes a factor that multiplies every neuron's rate multiplies the average too. That is the reassuring half of the lesson, and a good reason to report rate ratios for counts. The unreassuring half comes with the wire.

::: {.callout-tip title="How to read this figure"}
Each row is one *reading* of the same fitted model, not a different model. Compare the rows within a panel: if they agree, the choice of reading does not matter for this quantity; if they disagree, the sentence you write must say which one you mean. In the left panel they disagree by a factor of about two, so "the neurons fired at about X spikes per second" is a sentence with two possible values. In the right panel they disagree in the same proportion. The mistake to avoid is to report the intercept of a model with levels as "the mean rate".
:::

So there are two sentences a paper can write, and they are not interchangeable. If the claim is about *a neuron* ("a typical neuron fires 2% less per extra picture") the conditional effect is the right one. If the claim is about *the population* ("the average firing rate of hippocampal neurons changes by this much in spikes per second") the marginal one is, and for a log link the two agree on ratios and disagree on differences and on levels. I will write them both down at the end of the chapter.


## A predictor that varies between neurons {#sec-glmm-hemisphere}

@sec-count-regression left one prediction on the table. Load changes *within* every neuron, and there the pooled model was too cautious, because comparing a neuron with itself removes the differences between neurons. For a predictor that differs *between* neurons I said I expected the opposite, that pooling would be overconfident, and I promised to check. The hippocampus recordings have the right predictor for the check: each neuron was recorded in the left or in the right hemisphere (`hemisphere`), and a neuron cannot be in both. So the hemisphere is an attribute of the neuron, and the question *do hippocampal neurons of the left and right hemisphere fire at different rates?* is a comparison between neurons, with a design twist: most patients contributed neurons from one hemisphere only, so it is partly a comparison between patients too.
This check needs no posterior, only the contrast between the two ways of counting the rows, so I use the maximum-likelihood fits of `glmmTMB` (the Bayesian fit of the model with levels on 24,462 rows takes hours, and the answer would not change a sentence of what follows).








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Pooled model and model with levels for the hemisphere of the neuron, by maximum likelihood
spk$hemi <- factor(spk$hemisphere, levels = c("right", "left"))
glmm_hemi_pooled <- glmmTMB(spikes ~ hemi + offset(log(window_s)), family = nbinom2, data = spk)
glmm_hemi_ml <- glmmTMB(spikes ~ hemi + offset(log(window_s)) + (1 | patient) + (1 | neuron),
                        family = nbinom2, data = spk)

# Rate ratio (left against right) and Wald 95% interval from each model
hemi_row <- function(m, label) {
  s <- summary(m)$coefficients$cond["hemileft", ]
  data.frame(model = label, ratio = exp(s["Estimate"]),
             lo = exp(s["Estimate"] - 1.96 * s["Std. Error"]), hi = exp(s["Estimate"] + 1.96 * s["Std. Error"]))
}
hemi_df <- rbind(hemi_row(glmm_hemi_pooled, "Pooled (neurons ignored)"),
                 hemi_row(glmm_hemi_ml, "Patients and neurons as levels"))
hemi_df$model <- factor(hemi_df$model, levels = rev(hemi_df$model))
hemi_txt <- function(i) sprintf("%.2f [%.2f, %.2f]", hemi_df$ratio[i], hemi_df$lo[i], hemi_df$hi[i])
```
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Point and interval for the hemisphere effect under each model
ggplot(hemi_df, aes(x = ratio, y = model)) +
  geom_pointrange(aes(xmin = lo, xmax = hi), size = 0.7) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed", color = "grey40") +
  scale_x_log10(breaks = c(0.5, 0.7, 1, 1.4, 2)) +
  labs(x = "Rate ratio, left against right (log scale)", y = NULL) +
  Plot_theme +
  legend_none()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Firing-rate ratio of neurons recorded in the left hemisphere against the right, from the model that pools all neuron-trials and from the model with patients and neurons as levels (maximum likelihood; points and 95% Wald intervals). The dashed line is no difference.](multilevel-glmm_files/figure-html/fig-glmm_hemi-1.png){#fig-glmm_hemi fig-alt='Two horizontal intervals on a logarithmic axis around a dashed line at 1. The pooled model gives a narrow interval just below 1 that excludes it; the model with levels gives a much wider interval that includes it.' width=672}
:::
:::








@fig-glmm_hemi is the check, and it is as stark as I hoped. The pooled model, which treats the 24,462 trials as independent, puts the hemisphere effect at 0.94 [0.91, 0.97], an interval that excludes 1, and a reader of a spreadsheet would write that left-hemisphere neurons fire about 6% slower. The model that knows that trials come from neurons and neurons from patients says 0.90 [0.58, 1.40]: the interval is 13 times as wide on the log scale and includes 1. The number of independent pieces of evidence for a hemisphere effect is not 24,462, and it is not even 190: it is closer to the number of patients, who differ among themselves by a factor of about 2.1 per standard deviation. This is the pseudoreplication of @sec-multilevel-varying-intercepts again, in a count model, and it is the direction I predicted: for a predictor that varies between groups, ignoring the groups makes the interval too narrow.


## Can we trust the model? {#sec-glmm-checks}

The checks are the ones of @sec-mcmc-model-checking and @sec-count-regression, in the same order: whether the model reproduces the data it saw, at each level; whether it predicts what you want to predict; and whether the sampler can be believed.

### Predictive checks, at three levels

At the level of trials I use the rootogram and the share of zeros of @sec-count-regression (read them there if you need a refresher: bars hang from the expected counts, and a bar that stops short of the line means the model expected more of that count than we saw), now for the model with levels.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Statistic for the zero check: the share of trials with no spikes
zeros <- function(x) mean(x == 0)

# Two checks side by side: rootogram of the counts and the share of zeros
ppc_pair <- function(fit) {
  p1 <- pp_check(fit, type = "rootogram", style = "hanging", prob = 0.95, ndraws = 500) +
    coord_cartesian(xlim = c(0, 40)) +
    labs(title = "Rootogram", x = "Spikes in the window")
  p2 <- pp_check(fit, type = "stat", stat = "zeros", ndraws = 500) +
    labs(title = "Share of zeros", x = "Proportion of trials with 0 spikes")
  p1 + p2 + plot_layout(widths = c(1.4, 1))
}
```
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Run both checks on the slope model
ppc_pair(fit_spk_slope)
```

