# Maximum-likelihood fits with the same levels: negative binomial, and with a zero-inflation part
glmm_nb_ml <- glmmTMB(spikes ~ load_f + offset(log(window_s)) + (1 | patient) + (1 | neuron),
                      family = nbinom2, data = spk)
glmm_zinb <- glmmTMB(spikes ~ load_f + offset(log(window_s)) + (1 | patient) + (1 | neuron),
                     ziformula = ~ 1, family = nbinom2, data = spk)
```
:::








@fig-glmm_ppc_trials is the check of the family, and it does not pass cleanly. The rootogram is good: the bars hang close to the line across the counts, so the model describes how many spikes a window holds. The panel on the right is not: the observed share of zeros is 22%, and the model with levels generates 18% in its replicates, with the observed value outside all of them. The same check by patient (@fig-glmm_ppc_zeros) shows that it is not one patient: in most of them the observed share of empty trials lies to the right of the replicates. This is the opposite of the conclusion of @sec-count-regression, where three patients and a pooled model found nothing to inflate, and I owe you the reason I now think that was a matter of resolution. With neurons as a level, the model accounts for the slow neurons, which was the explanation of the zeros there, and what is left over is a small excess that the neuron baselines cannot explain. A zero-inflated negative binomial with the same levels, fitted by `glmmTMB`, puts the probability of a structural zero at 0.031 (about 3.1% of trials), lowers the AIC by 442 units and leaves the load coefficient where it was (-0.021 for three pictures against -0.023). So: a few percent of the windows are empty for a reason other than a slow neuron (a lapse of attention, an electrode that drifted), the question of the chapter does not depend on it, and the right thing to say in a paper is that the negative binomial slightly under-predicts empty windows. I did not build the zero-inflated model in `brms` (it would add a third hour-long fit), and it is the first thing I would add if the zeros were the question. The same check by patient (@fig-glmm_ppc_zeros) is below.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# The same zero check, patient by patient
pp_check(fit_spk_slope, type = "stat_grouped", stat = "zeros", group = "patient", ndraws = 500) +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(size = 8), strip.text = element_text(size = 10))
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Share of trials with no spikes in each of the 16 patients, in replicated data (histograms) against the observed share (line), from the slope model. Patient labels are on the panels.](multilevel-glmm_files/figure-html/fig-glmm_ppc_zeros-1.png){#fig-glmm_ppc_zeros fig-alt='Sixteen small panels, one per patient, each with a histogram of replicated zero shares and a vertical line for the observed share. The line falls inside or near the histogram in most panels.' width=864}
:::
:::








At the level of *neurons* the question is different, because the varying intercepts of the observed neurons can reproduce the observed neurons by construction. What the model does assume is that the baselines of *new* neurons come from a lognormal distribution, and I can check that by asking the fitted model to generate a fresh set of 190 neurons in the same patients and comparing the distribution of their firing rates with the observed one (@fig-glmm_ppc_neurons).








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# New neurons in the same patients: rename every neuron so that the model has never seen it
new_spk <- spk
new_spk$neuron <- factor(paste0("new_", spk$neuron))
set.seed(2)
yrep_new <- posterior_predict(fit_spk_slope, newdata = new_spk, allow_new_levels = TRUE,
                              sample_new_levels = "gaussian", ndraws = 200)

# Firing rate of each neuron in each replicated dataset: its spikes over its seconds
neuron_rows <- split(seq_len(nrow(spk)), spk$neuron)
rep_rate <- sapply(neuron_rows, function(i) rowSums(yrep_new[, i, drop = FALSE]) / sum(spk$window_s[i]))
rep_log10 <- log10(pmax(rep_rate, 0.01))
obs_log10 <- log10(pmax(sapply(neuron_rows, function(i) sum(spk$spikes[i]) / sum(spk$window_s[i])), 0.01))
```
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Observed distribution of neuron rates against replicated ones
bayesplot::ppc_dens_overlay(y = obs_log10, yrep = rep_log10[1:100, ]) +
  labs(x = "log10 firing rate (spikes per second)", y = "Density") +
  Plot_theme +
  legend_none()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Firing rate of the 190 neurons (log scale), as observed (dark curve) and in 100 replicated sets of new neurons in the same patients (faint curves). Rates below 0.01 spikes per second are set to 0.01.](multilevel-glmm_files/figure-html/fig-glmm_ppc_neurons-1.png){#fig-glmm_ppc_neurons fig-alt='A dark observed density over log10 firing rate, with a peak near 0.2 and a long right tail, inside a bundle of faint replicated densities of similar shape and spread.' width=576}
:::
:::








The replicated neurons spread over the same range as the observed ones and the shape is close, which is the assumption of normal baselines on the log scale doing its job. Where the faint curves and the dark one disagree is where I would look next, and I would look at the neurons themselves: a lognormal population is a model of *most* neurons, and a few very fast ones are the natural place for it to strain. I did not find enough of a disagreement to change the model.

### Cross-validation that matches the question {#sec-glmm-cv}

@sec-multilevel-varying-slopes warned that leave-one-out cross-validation on rows answers the question "how well do I predict a held-out row of a group I have already seen", and that this is not the only question. For the neurons it is worth spelling out, because three different questions are hiding in the data, and each wants a different held-out piece. @tbl-glmm_questions lists them.








::: {#tbl-glmm_questions .cell tbl-cap='Three questions a model with levels can be asked about a neuron, what the prediction is conditional on, and the cross-validation that matches each.' tbl-colwidths='[30,35,35]'}

```{.r .cell-code}
# The three questions, the draws they need and the matching cross-validation
q_tab <- data.frame(
  `The question` = c("A new trial of a neuron already recorded", "A new neuron of a patient already recorded",
                     "A neuron of a patient never recorded"),
  `What the prediction uses` = c("The neuron's own estimated baseline (the default)",
                                 "The patient's baseline and the spread of neurons (new levels for the neuron)",
                                 "Only the population baseline and both spreads (new levels for both)"),
  `Cross-validation that matches` = c("Leave one trial out (ordinary leave-one-out)",
                                      "Leave one neuron out (grouped folds that hold every neuron whole)",
                                      "Leave one patient out (folds that hold every patient whole)"),
  check.names = FALSE
)
gt(q_tab)
```

