Think of an exam. Ordinary leave-one-out is like grading a student on a question from a chapter that student already studied with the answer key: the model has seen the neuron's other trials and its prediction for the held-out one is easy. Leave-one-neuron-out is like a question about a chapter the student has not opened: the model must predict a neuron from what it learned about neurons in general. The two can rank the same models differently, and which ranking matters depends on whether your next experiment records the same neurons again (it almost never does) or new ones. The ladder of @fig-glmm_ladder shows the same three questions as three predictions of the firing rate of one neuron, so that you can see the price of each in the width of the interval.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Draws for patient 18, and for the neuron of that patient closest to its median rate
dr2 <- dr_ml
ex_neurons <- levels(spk$neuron)[grepl("^18_", levels(spk$neuron))]
ex_rate <- sapply(ex_neurons, function(k) sum(spk$spikes[spk$neuron == k]) / sum(spk$window_s[spk$neuron == k]))
ex_neuron <- ex_neurons[which.min(abs(ex_rate - median(ex_rate)))]

# Rate with one picture for: that neuron, a new neuron of patient 18 and a neuron of a new patient
lp_known <- dr2$b_Intercept + dr2[["r_patient[18,Intercept]"]] + dr2[[paste0("r_neuron[", ex_neuron, ",Intercept]")]]
set.seed(3)
lp_new_neuron <- dr2$b_Intercept + dr2[["r_patient[18,Intercept]"]] + rnorm(nrow(dr2), 0, dr2$sd_neuron__Intercept)
lp_new_patient <- dr2$b_Intercept + rnorm(nrow(dr2), 0, dr2$sd_patient__Intercept) + rnorm(nrow(dr2), 0, dr2$sd_neuron__Intercept)
ladder <- rbind(
  data.frame(who = "A neuron already recorded", rate = exp(lp_known)),
  data.frame(who = "A new neuron of the same patient", rate = exp(lp_new_neuron)),
  data.frame(who = "A neuron of a new patient", rate = exp(lp_new_patient))
)
ladder$who <- factor(ladder$who, levels = rev(unique(ladder$who)))
```
:::

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Half-eye of the rate for each of the three questions
ggplot(ladder, aes(x = rate, y = who)) +
  stat_halfeye(point_interval = median_qi, .width = c(0.66, 0.95), fill = "grey75") +
  scale_x_log10(breaks = c(0.1, 1, 10, 100)) +
  labs(x = "Firing rate (spikes per second, log scale)", y = NULL) +
  Plot_theme +
  legend_none()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Posterior of the firing rate (one picture on screen) for a neuron already recorded, for a new neuron of the same patient and for a neuron of a new patient, from the model with levels. The intervals are the 66% and 95% credible intervals and the axis is logarithmic.](multilevel-glmm_files/figure-html/fig-glmm_ladder-1.png){#fig-glmm_ladder fig-alt='Three half-eye densities on a logarithmic axis. The first is narrow around 1.5 spikes per second, the second is wide from 0.4 to 10, and the third is the widest from 0.2 to 20.' width=672}
:::
:::








@fig-glmm_ladder is the cost of each question made visible. A neuron already recorded has a rate known to within a few percent. A new neuron of the same patient is a draw from the patient's distribution of neurons, and its rate could plausibly be anything from 0.1 to 7.4 spikes per second. A neuron of a new patient is wider still, by the spread between patients. The same neuron, the same model, three different intervals, and all three are correct for their question. (The weather in your city again: the average temperature in October, the temperature tomorrow, and the temperature in a city you have not visited.) The books draw the same line: leave-one-out predicts a new observation of a group already in the data, and leave-one-group-out or grouped K-fold predicts a new group [@vehtari2017; @gelman2026]. They add a practical warning: the importance-sampling shortcut of leave-one-out tends to fail when a whole group is removed (Pareto $k$ above 0.7), because dropping a group moves the posterior of the spreads, and the remedies are grouped K-fold, an exact refit or moment matching.

I compute ordinary leave-one-out for the models of the chapter, because it is cheap, and I have to be honest about the other kind. The grouped version, which holds neurons whole, needs a refit for every fold, and one fit of the model with patients and neurons took about two hours on this machine; ten folds would take about a day, so I did not run it. The code is below, so that you can run it on your own data (with `eval: false` it is shown and not executed). It builds the folds by hand with `loo::kfold_split_grouped()`, because `brms` checks the grouping factor against every model and the pooled one has no neuron term. Be aware of what the comparison that follows can and cannot say: it predicts a held-out *trial* of a neuron the model has already seen.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Ten folds of neurons: every neuron is in exactly one fold, so a held-out fold holds neurons the model has never seen
set.seed(10)
folds_neuron <- loo::kfold_split_grouped(K = 10, x = spk$neuron)

# Each fold refits the model without its neurons (slow: about ten times the cost of one fit)
kf_neuron <- kfold(fit_spk_pooled, fit_spk_ml, folds = folds_neuron,
                   model_names = c("Neurons ignored", "Patients and neurons"))
```
:::

::: {#tbl-glmm_loo .cell tbl-cap='Ordinary leave-one-out comparison of the models for the spike counts (predicting a held-out trial of a neuron already seen). Differences are in elpd units relative to the best model, with the number of trials whose Pareto k exceeds 0.7.'}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Ordinary leave-one-out for the three models of the chapter (1,000 draws each, to keep memory small)
loo_models <- list("Neurons ignored" = fit_spk_pooled, "Patients and neurons" = fit_spk_ml,
                   "Varying slope of load" = fit_spk_slope)
loos <- lapply(loo_models, loo, ndraws = 1000)
cmp_ord <- loo_compare(loos)
k_bad <- vapply(loos, function(l) sum(loo::pareto_k_values(l) > 0.7), numeric(1))

# One row per model
tab_loo <- data.frame(Model = rownames(cmp_ord),
                      `elpd difference` = round(cmp_ord[, "elpd_diff"]),
                      `SE of difference` = round(cmp_ord[, "se_diff"]),
                      `Pareto k above 0.7` = unname(k_bad[rownames(cmp_ord)]), check.names = FALSE)
gt(tab_loo)
```

