@tbl-glmm_loo says what ordinary leave-one-out can say: the models that know about neurons predict a held-out trial far better than the pooled model (15,540 elpd units behind the best, against a standard error of 175), which is a statement about neurons we have already recorded and no more. The two models with levels differ by 1 elpd units, which is within noise, which fits what the slope model found: the responses differ so little that predicting a trial is no easier with them. What the table cannot say is how well each model would predict a neuron nobody has recorded, and for that question the pooled model, which describes every neuron with one rate, would fare worse than its place in this table suggests, because it has no way of knowing that neurons differ.

One alternative for the overdispersion deserves a paragraph and not a model. The negative binomial writes the trial-to-trial wobble of the rate as a gamma distribution; the other classical choice is a Poisson with an [observation-level random effect]{style="color:green; font-weight:bold;"}, one varying intercept per row of the data, which makes the wobble lognormal. It is the same idea (a rate that varies from trial to trial) with a different shape for the variation. A model with 24,462 extra parameters is not something I want to hand to `brms`, but `glmmTMB` fits it in seconds, and the comparison is informative *Bayesian Workflow* describes the varying intercept per observation as a common way to model overdispersion, reminds the reader that the negative binomial is a Poisson-gamma mixture, and observes that the normal model on the log scale is usually easier to understand and to build upon, for example when group-level predictors are added [@gelman2026]..








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Poisson with a lognormal rate for every trial (an observation-level random effect), by maximum likelihood
spk$obs <- factor(seq_len(nrow(spk)))
glmm_olre <- glmmTMB(spikes ~ load_f + offset(log(window_s)) + (1 | patient) + (1 | neuron) + (1 | obs),
                     family = poisson, data = spk)
```
:::








With the same predictors and the same two levels, the Poisson with a trial-level random effect has an AIC 120 units lower than the negative binomial, so the lognormal shape describes the trial-to-trial wobble of these neurons a little better than the gamma. It changes nothing that matters for the question: the coefficient for three pictures against one is -0.016 on the log scale against -0.023, and the spreads between patients and neurons are 0.75 and 1.02 against 0.74 and 1.01. I keep the negative binomial because it carries the continuity with @sec-count-regression and its shape has a reading (@eq-cv-nb), and I mention the alternative because a reviewer may ask.
## Counting neurons in tissue, with the mouse as a level {#sec-glmm-neun}

The first multilevel chapter modeled the density of NeuN-positive neurons with a Gaussian on the log scale and said, twice, that the family that respects that these are counts with an area was left for this chapter (@sec-multilevel-varying-intercepts, @sec-multilevel-varying-slopes). Here it is. It is the same cortex data (396 regions, 198 sections, 31 mice), the same predictors (day, hemisphere and their interaction) and the same structure (a varying intercept for the mouse and one for the section), with the family of @sec-count-regression: a negative binomial with the log of the area, in mm², as the offset, so that the coefficients are log density ratios as before. There was no reason to expect a large change: the counts are in the hundreds or thousands, far from zero.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# The NeuN counts of the first multilevel chapter, as counts with the section area as an exposure
fit_neun_nb <- brm(
  positive ~ dpi * hemi + offset(log(area_mm2)) + (1 | mouse) + (1 | section),
  data = cortex, family = negbinomial(), prior = prior_neun_nb,
  chains = 4, cores = 4, seed = 123, iter = 4000, control = list(adapt_delta = 0.99),
  file = "models/multilevel-glmm_fit10.rds", file_refit = "never"
)
```
