## Nine attempts, one mouse: a logistic model with levels {#sec-glmm-wire-logit}

Back to the wire, and to the promise of @sec-beta-robust-regression that the overdispersion of the nine attempts would stop being a parameter buried in a beta-binomial and become something I could look at. The attempts are the rows. A mouse made nine of them (three on each of three days), each scored as completed or not, and what the beta-binomial said is that the nine are *not* nine independent coin flips of one probability: some mice are simply better at the wire than others. A varying intercept says the same thing in a different way. Each mouse gets its own log-odds of completing an attempt, drawn from a population of mice with a spread, and the attempts of that mouse are coin flips at its own probability:

$$
y_{ij} \sim \text{Bernoulli}(p_{ij}), \qquad \text{logit}(p_{ij}) = \alpha + \beta\, \text{KO}_j + u_j, \qquad u_j \sim \text{Normal}(0,\, \tau_{\text{mouse}}).
$$ {#eq-glmm-wire-logit}

The coefficient $\beta$ is the genotype effect on the log-odds for a mouse at the middle of its genotype's distribution, and $\tau_{\text{mouse}}$ is the number I am after. The prior for $\tau_{\text{mouse}}$ needs thought, because on the logit scale a standard deviation of 3 is not absurd: it describes mice whose odds of completing an attempt differ by a factor of $e^3 = 20$ per standard deviation. I give the intercept a `Normal(2, 1.5)` (a typical mouse completes about nine attempts in ten), the genotype effect the `Normal(0, 1.5)` of @sec-beta-robust-regression, and the spread an Exponential(0.5), a mean of 2, wide enough that the data will not be told how different the mice are. The prior predictive check asks whether such priors can produce what the wire experiment looks like, the number of completed attempts out of nine across 63 mice (@fig-glmm_wire_prior).








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Prior predictive fit for the number of completed attempts (priors only, no likelihood)
fit_wire_prior <- brm(
  success ~ genotype + (1 | mouse),
  data = attempts, family = bernoulli(), prior = prior_wire_logit, sample_prior = "only",
  chains = 4, cores = 4, seed = 123,
  file = "models/multilevel-glmm_fit11.rds", file_refit = "never"
)

# Attempt-level logistic model that ignores which mouse made the attempt
fit_wire_pooled <- brm(
  success ~ genotype,
  data = attempts, family = bernoulli(), prior = prior_wire_logit[prior_wire_logit$class != "sd", ],
  chains = 4, cores = 4, seed = 123,
  file = "models/multilevel-glmm_fit12.rds", file_refit = "never"
)

# The same with a varying intercept for each mouse
fit_wire_logit <- brm(
  success ~ genotype + (1 | mouse),
  data = attempts, family = bernoulli(), prior = prior_wire_logit,
  chains = 4, cores = 4, seed = 123, iter = 4000, control = ml_control,
  file = "models/multilevel-glmm_fit13.rds", file_refit = "never"
)

# Adding the day of training
fit_wire_logit_day <- brm(
  success ~ genotype + day_c + (1 | mouse),
  data = attempts, family = bernoulli(), prior = prior_wire_logit,
  chains = 4, cores = 4, seed = 123, iter = 4000, control = ml_control,
  file = "models/multilevel-glmm_fit14.rds", file_refit = "never"
)

# Sensitivity to the prior of the spread between mice
fit_wire_logit_narrow <- brm(
  success ~ genotype + (1 | mouse),
  data = attempts, family = bernoulli(), prior = prior_wire_logit_narrow,
  chains = 4, cores = 4, seed = 123, iter = 4000, control = ml_control,
  file = "models/multilevel-glmm_fit15.rds", file_refit = "never"
)
fit_wire_logit_wide <- brm(
  success ~ genotype + (1 | mouse),
  data = attempts, family = bernoulli(), prior = prior_wire_logit_wide,
  chains = 4, cores = 4, seed = 123, iter = 4000, control = ml_control,
  file = "models/multilevel-glmm_fit16.rds", file_refit = "never"
