The spread between mice is 2.4 [1.6, 3.5] on the logit scale. That is large, and it is worth saying what it means: a mouse one standard deviation above the typical one of its genotype has odds of completing an attempt about 11 times higher, and in terms of probabilities it means that most mice are at the ceiling and a few are nowhere near it, which is the shape of @fig-beta_ppc_counts_both. The genotype coefficient is -1.69 [-3.26, -0.21] for the logistic model with levels and -1.35 [-2.10, -0.70] for the pooled one: the interval is 2.2 times as wide when the mice are a level, which is the direction the wire promised (the genotype differs *between* mice, and the pooled model counted each mouse nine times). And it is a different number from the -1.33 [-2.55, -0.29] of the beta-binomial, which looks wrong until we see why.

### Two descriptions of one overdispersion {#sec-glmm-rho}

@sec-beta-robust-regression turned the mouse-to-mouse spread into $\phi$ and then into $\rho = 1/(\phi+1)$, the correlation between two attempts of the same mouse. The logistic model with a varying intercept turns the same spread into $\tau_{\text{mouse}}$. They are two descriptions of one fact, and to compare them I need to put them on the same scale. There are two natural ones. The *observed* scale is the one of $\rho$: the correlation between the outcomes of two attempts of the same mouse. For the logistic model it is the variance of the mouse's probability, divided by $\mu(1-\mu)$ with $\mu$ the average probability (the same thing $\rho$ is for a beta distribution), and it can be computed by averaging over the distribution of mice. The *latent* scale is the one of the linear predictor: the share of the variance of the log-odds that lies between mice. The logistic distribution has a variance of $\pi^2/3 \approx 3.3$ on that scale, which plays the role that the trigamma variance played for the negative binomial, so that the latent intraclass correlation is $\tau_{\text{mouse}}^2 / (\tau_{\text{mouse}}^2 + \pi^2/3)$ [@nakagawa2017].








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Correlation between two attempts of one mouse on the observed scale, for a genotype with log-odds eta0,
# averaging over a fixed set of 2000 quantiles of the mouse distribution
z <- qnorm(ppoints(2000))
obs_icc <- function(eta0, tau) {
  p <- plogis(eta0 + tau * z)
  mu <- mean(p)
  var(p) / (mu * (1 - mu))
}
idx <- round(seq(1, nrow(dr_w), length.out = 1000))
icc_wt <- mapply(function(a, t) obs_icc(a, t), dr_w$b_Intercept[idx], sd_m[idx])
icc_ko <- mapply(function(a, b, t) obs_icc(a + b, t), dr_w$b_Intercept[idx], dr_w$b_genotypeKO[idx], sd_m[idx])

# Latent-scale correlation, and the correlation of the beta-binomial of the chapter on behavior
icc_latent <- sd_m^2 / (sd_m^2 + pi^2 / 3)
rho_bb <- 1 / (dr_bb$phi + 1)

rho_df <- rbind(
  data.frame(what = "Latent scale (logistic)", value = icc_latent),
  data.frame(what = "Observed, wild type (logistic)", value = icc_wt),
  data.frame(what = "Observed, KO (logistic)", value = icc_ko),
  data.frame(what = "Observed (beta-binomial)", value = rho_bb)
)
rho_df$what <- factor(rho_df$what, levels = rev(unique(rho_df$what)))
```
:::

::: {.cell}

