@fig-glmm_rho is the picture I wanted to give you. Read the bottom three rows first, because they are on the same scale: the beta-binomial said that two attempts of a mouse correlate at 0.30 [0.17, 0.48], and the logistic model with levels says 0.28 [0.11, 0.49] for the wild types and 0.38 [0.22, 0.54] for the KO mice. These are two descriptions of the same fact, reached by two different distributions of the mouse-to-mouse spread, and they overlap: the overdispersion of the wire is real, it does not depend on which family writes it down, and a mouse-level model *did* make explicit what was buried in $\phi$. Now read the top row. On the latent scale the same spread is a correlation of 0.63 [0.45, 0.79], more than twice as large. Nothing is wrong. The logit scale has a large variance of its own (the $\pi^2/3$ of every coin flip) and the mice are far apart on it, but most of that distance is squeezed away at the ceiling of the probability scale: a mouse at 99% and one at 99.9% differ by a factor of ten in the odds and by nothing in the number of attempts completed. This is why a latent intraclass correlation reported without its scale is a number nobody can check against the data, and why I give the scale each time.

::: {.callout-tip title="How to read this figure"}
Compare the positions of the half-eyes, not their shapes. Rows on the same scale (the three at the bottom) should agree if the families describe the same fact, and they do. The top row is on a different scale and answers a different question: *how much of the spread of the linear predictor is between mice*, not *how much two attempts of one mouse agree*. The mistake to avoid is to compare the two kinds of numbers.
:::

Two things are not the same between the two models, and they are worth knowing. The first is the *shape* of the mouse-to-mouse distribution: the beta-binomial writes it as a beta distribution of the probabilities, and the logistic as a normal distribution of the log-odds, which is the same thing near the middle and different at the ends. The second is what the correlation depends on: the beta-binomial has one correlation for both genotypes, and in the logistic model the observed-scale correlation changes with the mean (it differs a little between the wild types and the KO mice in @fig-glmm_rho), because probabilities near the ceiling have less room to differ. The books describe both as mixtures for overdispersed grouped binomial data and give nearly identical answers when the overdispersion is moderate; they part when it is extreme or the groups are small, because a beta distribution can be U-shaped, piling probability near 0 and 1, while a normal distribution of the log-odds is a bell on its own scale [@mcelreath2020; @inchausti2023]. Whether the data can tell the two shapes apart with 63 mice I doubt, and I have not tried.

### Conditional or marginal, again {#sec-glmm-wire-marginal}

Now the number I said looked wrong. The beta-binomial of @sec-beta-robust-regression gave a genotype coefficient of -1.33 and the logistic model with levels gives -1.69. They model the same data and describe the same overdispersion, and the coefficients differ by 27%. The reason is the one of the spike section, and here it bites. In the beta-binomial the logit link is applied to $\mu$, the *mean* of the probabilities of the mice of a genotype, so its coefficient is a log-odds ratio of *average* probabilities: a marginal quantity. In the logistic model with levels the logit link is applied to the probability of a mouse with $u_j = 0$, so its coefficient is the log-odds ratio *for a typical mouse*: a conditional one. With a ceiling and mice that differ this much, the two are far apart (the conditional coefficient is the larger in magnitude, as the books say it must be), and so are their predictions in the units a reader understands, as @fig-glmm_wire_marginal shows. I compute four numbers in percentage points of the probability of completing an attempt: the difference for a typical mouse (setting $u_j = 0$), the average over the 63 mice we studied (each mouse with its own estimated intercept, genotype switched, as in the average predictive comparisons of @sec-logistic-regression), the average over the population of mice (drawing new mice from the fitted spread), and, for reference, the beta-binomial and the pooled logistic.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Typical mouse: the intercept of each genotype with the mouse deviation set to zero
typical_pp <- 100 * (plogis(dr_w$b_Intercept + dr_w$b_genotypeKO) - plogis(dr_w$b_Intercept))

# Mice studied: every mouse with its own estimated intercept, as KO and as WT (fitted draws, 1000 of them)
ids <- round(seq(1, ndraws(fit_wire_logit), length.out = 1000))
wire_mice <- unique(attempts[, c("mouse", "genotype")])
nd_ko <- transform(wire_mice, genotype = factor("KO", levels = c("WT", "KO")))
nd_wt <- transform(wire_mice, genotype = factor("WT", levels = c("WT", "KO")))
studied_pp <- 100 * rowMeans(posterior_epred(fit_wire_logit, newdata = nd_ko, draw_ids = ids) -
                               posterior_epred(fit_wire_logit, newdata = nd_wt, draw_ids = ids))

# Population of mice: average the probability over a fixed set of quantiles of the mouse distribution
avg_p <- function(eta0, tau) mean(plogis(eta0 + tau * z))
population_pp <- 100 * mapply(function(a, b, t) avg_p(a + b, t) - avg_p(a, t),
                              dr_w$b_Intercept[ids], dr_w$b_genotypeKO[ids], sd_m[ids])

# The beta-binomial (mean probabilities) and the pooled logistic have no mouse deviations
bb_pp <- 100 * (plogis(dr_bb$b_Intercept + dr_bb$b_genotypeKO) - plogis(dr_bb$b_Intercept))
pooled_pp <- 100 * (plogis(dr_wp$b_Intercept + dr_wp$b_genotypeKO) - plogis(dr_wp$b_Intercept))

marg_wire <- rbind(
  data.frame(what = "Logistic, typical mouse", value = typical_pp),
  data.frame(what = "Logistic with levels, the 63 mice studied", value = studied_pp),
  data.frame(what = "Logistic with levels, population of mice", value = population_pp),
  data.frame(what = "Beta-binomial", value = bb_pp),
  data.frame(what = "Logistic, mice ignored", value = pooled_pp)
)
marg_wire$what <- factor(marg_wire$what, levels = rev(unique(marg_wire$what)))
```
