@fig-glmm_wire_marginal makes the difference visible. For a typical mouse, the genotype changes the probability of completing an attempt by -2.6 [-7.7, -0.2] percentage points, a small number, because a typical wild-type mouse is already at the ceiling (99.4%) and a typical KO mouse is almost there too. Averaged over mice, whether the 63 we studied (-8.1 [-14.2, -0.6]) or the population they come from (-8.2 [-15.9, -0.6]), the difference is about 3 times larger. The reason is the ceiling again: the average mouse is not the typical mouse, because a good mouse has nothing to lose and a poorer one has a great deal, and the ones with something to lose are the ones that carry the average. The beta-binomial (-8.6 [-16.5, -2.0]) and the pooled logistic (-9.3 [-13.7, -5.0]) agree with the marginal readings, because they model the average probabilities directly. And the intervals: the pooled model is the narrowest, the models with levels are wider (a 95% interval 15 points wide for the population of mice, against 9 for the pooled one), because the genotype is a property of the mouse and the model with levels knows that the 567 attempts are 63 mice.

::: {.callout-tip title="How to read this figure"}
All five rows answer *how much does KO change the probability of completing an attempt*, and they answer it for five different kinds of mouse. Check the first row against the rest: if a model with levels gives a conditional effect that is much smaller than the marginal one, the distribution of mice has a ceiling or a floor in it, and the conditional effect is a statement about a mouse that is atypical in the way that matters. The mistake to avoid is to report the odds ratio of a logistic model with levels as "the effect of KO on the probability" without saying *for which mouse*.
:::

Which of the five should a paper report? I would report the marginal difference, as the headline, because it answers the question a reader is likely to ask (*what fraction of attempts does a KO mouse fail that a wild-type mouse does not?*), and give the odds ratio for the typical mouse as the model coefficient, labelled as conditional. The two sentences are in the reporting box at the end of the chapter.








