:::








The check of @fig-glmm_wire_ppc is the one of @fig-beta_ppc_counts_both, with a twist that matters. A model with a varying intercept can reproduce the 63 mice we have trivially, because each mouse has its own parameter; the fair question is whether the *distribution* of mice that the model has learned can generate a sample that looks like the real one. So the replicated datasets are for 63 *new* mice, drawn from the fitted spread. The band covers the bars, the perfect scores included, which is what the binomial could not do and what the beta-binomial could. The two families that describe the mouse-to-mouse spread agree here too.

::: {.callout-tip title="Frequentist equivalent"}
`glmmTMB` agrees with `brms` that the mice differ a lot and that the genotype effect on the typical mouse is negative, but it gets there with less stability. Its estimate of the spread between mice is 3 and its intercept is 6.4 on the logit scale, where the posterior medians are 2.4 and 5: with most mice at the ceiling, the likelihood is nearly flat in the direction of "a bigger spread and a higher typical probability", and a maximum-likelihood fit drifts along it, with a Wald standard error for the intercept of 1.2. The prior on the spread is doing something practical here, not a philosophical one, and the sensitivity table in the section on few groups says how much. The maximum-likelihood coefficient for genotype is -2.36 against the posterior median -1.69.
:::

## Repairing the crossing times {#sec-glmm-wire-t}

The second debt. The Student-*t* of @sec-beta-robust-regression, on the log of the crossing time of every completed attempt, gave an interval for genotype that I said was too narrow, because it counted 516 attempts as independent and they came from 63 mice. The repair is a varying intercept for each mouse, and the question that makes the repair informative, as in @sec-multilevel-varying-intercepts, is *which* comparisons it changes and in which direction. Genotype is a property of the mouse: it varies between mice, so I expect the interval to widen. The day of training varies within every mouse (each mouse was tested on all three days), so I expect it to narrow, because each mouse is compared with itself.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Student-t on log crossing time, ignoring mice (the model of the chapter on behavior)
fit_wire_t <- brm(
  log_wire ~ genotype + day_c + attempt_c,
  data = crossings, family = student(), prior = prior_wire_t,
  chains = 4, cores = 4, seed = 123,
  file = "models/multilevel-glmm_fit17.rds", file_refit = "never"
)

# The same with a varying intercept for each mouse
fit_wire_t_ml <- brm(
  log_wire ~ genotype + day_c + attempt_c + (1 | mouse),
  data = crossings, family = student(), prior = prior_wire_t_ml,
  chains = 4, cores = 4, seed = 123, iter = 4000, control = ml_control,
  file = "models/multilevel-glmm_fit18.rds", file_refit = "never"
)

# Without the impossible record
fit_wire_t_ml_clean <- brm(
  log_wire ~ genotype + day_c + attempt_c + (1 | mouse),
  data = crossings_clean, family = student(), prior = prior_wire_t_ml,
  chains = 4, cores = 4, seed = 123, iter = 4000, control = ml_control,
  file = "models/multilevel-glmm_fit19.rds", file_refit = "never"
)
```
:::








I keep the 516 attempts of the chapter on behavior, including the one record whose total time is shorter than its time to get into position (mouse KO122, day 3): the Student-*t* absorbs it, as it did there, and a refit without that record is in the table below so that you can see what the decision cost. The priors are those of that chapter, plus an Exponential(3) for the spread between mice (a mean of a third on the log scale, about a 40% difference in crossing time per standard deviation).

