I drew @fig-glmm_t_compare as intervals on adjacent rows for the same reason as in @sec-multilevel-varying-intercepts: the point estimates barely move, and the *width* is the result. The genotype ratio is 1.30 [1.20, 1.41] when the attempts are treated as independent and 1.28 [1.09, 1.52] with the mouse as a level: the interval is 2 times as wide, which is what the chapter on behavior predicted and what the design effect said it should be. The practice effect goes the other way: 0.79 [0.75, 0.83] per day without the mouse and 0.79 [0.76, 0.82] with it, an interval 0.78 times as wide, which is to say narrower, because every mouse supplies its own comparison across days. The impossible record changes neither of them noticeably (1.29 [1.09, 1.52] for genotype without it).

::: {.callout-tip title="How to read this figure"}
Read a panel row by row, from top to bottom, and keep your eye on the dashed line at 1. The centers of the three rows should be close; if they are not, the structure is changing the answer, not only its precision. Then compare the lengths of the bars: a longer interval in the genotype panel and a shorter one in the day panel is exactly the signature of a varying intercept at work, wider where the comparison is *between* groups and narrower where it is *within* them. The mistake to avoid is to prefer the narrow interval of the first row because it looks more decisive.
:::

How much of the variation in crossing time is between mice? On the log scale the standard deviation between mice is 0.31 [0.25, 0.38] and the intraclass correlation (with the variance of a Student-*t* for the residual) is 0.41 [0.30, 0.53]: about 41% of the variance of the log crossing time is between mice, in the neighborhood of the 35% that the chapter on behavior estimated from a Gaussian model. A mouse is fast or slow, and its crossings are more like each other than like those of another mouse. The effect of genotype is thus a statement about 63 mice, not 516 attempts, and the model with the mouse as a level is the one that says so. The model of the previous chapter that lets each mouse have its own *practice* slope (@sec-multilevel-varying-slopes) is the next step in the same direction, and I do not repeat it.

::: {.callout-tip title="Frequentist equivalent"}
`glmmTMB` with a Student-*t* family agrees with `brms`: the genotype coefficient is 0.26 with a standard error of 0.082 against a posterior median of 0.25 and a posterior standard deviation of 0.083, and the spread between mice is 0.3 against 0.31. With 63 mice and 8 attempts each, the likelihood does the work and the weakly informative priors barely matter, so the two fits are numerically close. What `brms` adds is a whole posterior for the degrees of freedom $\nu$, which here is 6 [4, 12]: the data say little about the tails.
:::

## How many groups is enough? {#sec-glmm-fewgroups}

The opener said that a multilevel model needs enough groups, not just enough rows, and promised that the neurons would show it. I have been using 16 patients. @sec-count-regression used three, and the models above can be refit with three, the same priors and the same code, to see what it does to the spread between patients.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Three patients only: the model of fit_spk_ml, to see what a spread between patients looks like with three
