![Posterior of the spread between patients (standard deviation of the patient intercepts, log scale) with three patients and with sixteen, under the Exponential(2) prior (dashed). With three groups the data hardly move the prior; with sixteen they pin it down.](multilevel-glmm_files/figure-html/fig-glmm_fewgroups-1.png){#fig-glmm_fewgroups fig-alt='Two stacked density plots on a horizontal axis from 0 to 3. The upper one, for three patients, is broad and similar in shape to the dashed exponential prior curve, with a long right tail. The lower one, for sixteen patients, is narrow and peaked near 0.75.' width=672}
:::
:::








I drew @fig-glmm_fewgroups with the prior as a dashed line so that the reader can see the thing that matters for few groups, which is *how much the data moved the prior*. With three patients the posterior for the spread between patients is 0.70 [0.18, 1.77] (a long tail to the right, and a shape that is close to the prior's); with 16 patients it is 0.75 [0.48, 1.19], 2 times narrower. Three patients are three data points for $\tau_{\text{patient}}$, however many neurons each has, and the spread between neurons is the same in both: 1.03 [0.86, 1.26] with three patients, 1.02 [0.92, 1.13] with sixteen, because there are 59 and 190 neurons to say it. A maximum-likelihood fit does not warn you in a case like this one: `glmmTMB` returns 0.67 for the three patients, a reasonable-looking number, and only if you ask for an interval does it say 0.23 to 1.91. (With a still smaller number of groups, the usual symptom is an estimate of exactly zero and a warning of a singular fit, which is what @sec-multilevel-varying-slopes showed with a varying slope.) The numbers of the opener are the ones the books give: at least five levels with more than ten replicates for a linear model, and fewer than about fifteen clusters with fewer than ten rows each putting a binary GLMM at risk of non-convergence and biased variances [@inchausti2023]; and with very few groups and a broad prior the hierarchy produces almost no shrinkage [@kruschke2015]. The remedy they recommend is a weakly informative prior that regularizes toward zero with light tails (an exponential or a half-normal, as used here) and not a flat prior or an inverse-gamma with tiny parameters [@gelman2006; @gelman2026].

The remedy is not a cleverer model. It is more groups, or an honest statement of what three groups can tell you. A prior can help a little, by ruling out absurd spreads, and it can also dominate, so I check how much it does (@tbl-glmm_prior_sens): the same model with a narrow and a wide prior on the spreads (Exponential(5) and Exponential(0.5), against the Exponential(2) used), for the three patients, and the wire model with its own three. (I did not refit the 16-patient model under the other priors: each such fit takes about an hour, and with sixteen patients the posterior in the figure above is already far from its prior.)








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# One row per fit: spread between patients (or mice), spread between neurons, and the effect of interest
sens_row <- function(fit, label, data, par, effect) {
  d <- as_draws_df(fit)
  has_pat <- "sd_patient__Intercept" %in% names(d)
  data.frame(Data = data, Prior = label,
             `Spread between patients (log scale)` = if (has_pat) summ(d$sd_patient__Intercept) else NA,
             `Spread between neurons or mice` = summ(if (has_pat) d$sd_neuron__Intercept else d$sd_mouse__Intercept, 2),
             `Effect of interest` = effect(d[[par]]), check.names = FALSE)
}
rr_text <- function(x) summ(exp(x), 3)
or_text <- function(x) summ(x, 2)

sens_tab <- rbind(
  sens_row(fit_spk_3pat_narrow, "Exponential(5), narrow", "Spikes, 3 patients", "b_load_f3", rr_text),
  sens_row(fit_spk_3pat, "Exponential(2), used", "Spikes, 3 patients", "b_load_f3", rr_text),
  sens_row(fit_spk_3pat_wide, "Exponential(0.5), wide", "Spikes, 3 patients", "b_load_f3", rr_text),
  sens_row(fit_spk_ml, "Exponential(2), used", "Spikes, 16 patients", "b_load_f3", rr_text),
  sens_row(fit_wire_logit_narrow, "Exponential(2), narrow", "Wire, 63 mice", "b_genotypeKO", or_text),
  sens_row(fit_wire_logit, "Exponential(0.5), used", "Wire, 63 mice", "b_genotypeKO", or_text),
  sens_row(fit_wire_logit_wide, "Exponential(0.2), wide", "Wire, 63 mice", "b_genotypeKO", or_text)
)
```
:::

