All 15 pass: the worst Rhat is 1.006, the smallest effective sample size is 459 and there are 0 divergent transitions in total. They did not all pass the first time, and the failures are instructive. My first fit of the model with patients and neurons used the defaults and had 43 divergent transitions, an Rhat of 1.013 and an effective sample size of 190 for the spread between patients: with 16 patients the spread between them is the weakest-identified parameter of the model, and the patient and neuron levels compete to explain the same differences. A dense mass matrix, `adapt_delta = 0.95` and 2,000 iterations removed most of the trouble but not all (2 divergences, Rhat 1.019, an effective sample size of 239). What passed was `adapt_delta = 0.99`, a new seed and 6,000 iterations for that model, 4,000 iterations at 0.99 for the model with a varying slope, and `adapt_delta = 0.999` for the models of three patients with the default and the wide prior, whose wide prior produced 25 divergences at 0.99. The first NeuN count model needed 0.99 as well. I did not trust a posterior with divergences, as @sec-mcmc-model-checking asks, and this is what it costs: the model with patients and neurons took 2.6 hours and the one with the varying slope 5.2 hours (the latter while another job shared the processor).

## Reporting the results {#sec-glmm-report}

I give the two sentences for each of the two analyses: the one for the neurons and the one for the wire. In both I keep the two readings apart (conditional and marginal), because it is the habit of this chapter that matters most to carry out of it.

::: {.callout-note title="How to report it: the neurons"}
**Results.** In 190 hippocampal neurons of 16 patients (24,462 neuron-trials), the firing rate of a typical neuron changed by a factor of 0.988 [0.975, 1.001] for each additional picture held in memory (rate ratio; posterior median and 95% credible interval; conditional on a neuron with zero deviation). The ratio of the population-average rates was 0.989 [0.974, 1.002]. A typical neuron fired at 1.5 [1.0, 2.3] spikes per second with one picture and the average neuron of the population at 3.4 [2.2, 6.5]; from one to three pictures the average rate changed by -0.07 [-0.21, 0.02] spikes per second. Neurons differed little in their response (standard deviation of the slopes 0.037 [0.006, 0.058] on the log scale per picture) compared with their baselines (1.02 [0.92, 1.14]); 59% of the variance of the log rate lay between neurons of the same patient and 32% between patients (latent scale). Left-hemisphere neurons fired at 0.90 [0.58, 1.40] (95% Wald interval, maximum likelihood) times the rate of right-hemisphere neurons (a comparison between neurons and patients).

**Methods.** Spike counts in the encoding window were modeled with a negative binomial likelihood, a log link and the log of the window length as an offset, with a varying intercept for patient and a varying intercept and slope of load (number of pictures beyond the first) for each neuron, correlated. Priors were Normal(0.5, 1.5) for the intercept, Normal(0, 0.5) for the load coefficient, InvGamma(0.4, 0.3) for the shape, Exponential(2) for the spreads of the baselines, Exponential(4) for the spread of the slopes and LKJ(2) for their correlation. Four chains of 4,000 iterations (dense mass matrix, `adapt_delta` 0.99; 6,000 for the model without the slope) gave Rhat below 1.01, effective sample sizes above 400 and no divergent transitions. Marginal quantities average over the fitted distribution of neurons and patients. A zero-inflated version fitted by maximum likelihood put the probability of a structural zero at 0.031 and left the load effect unchanged. The spread between patients was estimated from 16 patients; with three it was close to its prior.
:::

::: {.callout-note title="How to report it: the wire"}
**Results.** Across 63 mice (28 wild type, 35 Klf4-KO) and 567 attempts, KO mice completed 8 [1, 16] percentage points fewer attempts than wild types (marginal difference, averaged over the population of mice; posterior median and 95% credible interval). For a typical mouse the odds of completing an attempt were multiplied by 0.19 [0.04, 0.81] (conditional odds ratio), a smaller change on the probability scale (2.6 [0.3, 7.7] points) because typical mice were near the ceiling. Mice differed greatly (standard deviation of the mouse intercepts 2.4 [1.6, 3.5] on the log-odds scale; correlation between two attempts of one mouse 0.28 [0.11, 0.49] on the observed scale in wild types). When completed, crossings took 28 [9, 52]% longer in KO mice (ratio of times conditional on mouse, day and attempt); 41% of the variance of the log time was between mice, and the interval for genotype was 2 times as wide as when attempts were treated as independent.

**Methods.** Completion was modeled with a logistic model for each attempt with a varying intercept for mouse (priors Normal(2, 1.5) for the intercept, Normal(0, 1.5) for genotype, Exponential(0.5) for the spread of mice) and crossing time with a Student-*t* model for the log time with the same structure (Exponential(3) for the spread of mice). Four chains of 4,000 iterations (`adapt_delta` 0.95) met the sampler criteria. Marginal differences averaged the probability over the fitted distribution of mice; the conditional odds ratio refers to a mouse with zero deviation. The spread of mice was sensitive to its prior (@tbl-glmm_prior_sens), the genotype effect much less. One record with a total time shorter than its time to get into position was kept; removing it changed the genotype ratio from 1.28 [1.09, 1.52] to 1.29 [1.09, 1.52].
:::

The two readings of one effect are the part of these paragraphs I would not let a coauthor delete. A reader who sees only "the odds ratio was 0.2" will picture a mouse that does not exist.

## What the chapter settled, and what comes next {#sec-glmm-close}

We started with a colleague's hand in the air and a debt. The hand asked whether a flat average was hiding neurons that speed up and neurons that slow down. The answer, with 190 neurons from 16 patients, is that the neurons do differ in their response, by a few percent per picture (@fig-glmm_slopes), against baselines that differ a hundredfold, and that most of the apparent spread of the raw slopes was the counting noise of single neurons. The debt was two intervals that were too narrow. The Student-*t* for the crossing times, with the mouse as a level, now has an interval for genotype about twice as wide, and a narrower one for the practice effect (@fig-glmm_t_compare); and the pooled hemisphere comparison that looked decisive for the spikes turned out to have far less evidence behind it than its 24,462 rows suggested (@fig-glmm_hemi). The promise of the first multilevel chapter, the NeuN counts with an area offset, came out in agreement with the log-Gaussian model (@fig-glmm_neun).

Four ideas are worth carrying out of the chapter. The family and the structure are two dials, and moving one does not change what the other means (@tbl-glmm_map). In a model with a log or a logit link the coefficient is a *conditional* effect, for a neuron or a mouse at the typical value of its deviation, and what a paper usually means is a *marginal* one, averaged over the population: for ratios and a small spread they nearly agree, and for probabilities near a ceiling they are far apart (@fig-glmm_spk_marginal, @fig-glmm_wire_marginal). A logistic model with a varying intercept and a beta-binomial are two descriptions of one overdispersion, and a correlation reported without its scale (latent or observed) cannot be checked against the data (@fig-glmm_rho). And what you want to predict, a new trial, a new neuron or a new patient, decides the cross-validation that is honest, and the width of the interval you should expect (@tbl-glmm_questions, @fig-glmm_ladder).

I leave three things open. A response to load that is not a straight line on the log scale, a neuron that rises with the second picture and falls with the third, is not something a single slope can see; the factor version of the varying effect would, and the data per neuron are thin for it. The outcomes of the wire and of the open field are still treated as if the days were exchangeable or independent, which is false in the way that time makes it false. And a model for *graded* outcomes, where the categories have an order, is a family this book has not met. The next chapters take these up in turn: the ordered and unordered categories of neurological deficit (@sec-ordinal-nominal-regression), the variance that has been a single $\sigma$ so far (@sec-multivariate-distributional-regression), and the animals followed through time, the open-field mice on six days and the full course of the glial scar, where the sessions of one animal depend on each other, as a trait of the animal or as a memory of the last session (@sec-longitudinal-timeseries).
